 - Where the full-resolution GPU frame goes (M4 Pro, Metal System Trace, 2026-11-08): vertex/mesh 4.9, fragment 5.2, compute 4.3 ms per 1/60 s, and they do not overlap (their union is 14.4 ms, their sum 14.4). The heaviest passes: the main mesh pass (task `a5288d0a6c4971fd`, mesh `3439aaa071d868ae`, fragment `a1a56cf6d62ae798`), two foliage passes (task `47f9b38490dcf9a2`), and the upscaler's compute (`fa3033d05c0a41d6`, 177 HMMA calls, already on `simdgroup` matrices). Ruled out: the stage serialization is mostly the game's own barriers (about 70 a frame, 39 of them `ORDER_PRIMITIVES | INVALIDATE_TEXTURE`). The relaxed draw-pass barriers of `draw_barriers game` were then off whenever an image family heap existed (this game's dynamic-resolution depth buffer makes one). Keeping them on with the alias flush moved to a start barrier skipped 22% of the full barriers, but the real GPU span on this M4 Pro did not change (14.7 ms p50 either way, two runs each). That change is on main now, with fragment-only barriers no longer forcing the full one, for its gain on the M1 Max ([Draw passes that wait for each other again while images share memory](../../../docs/reference/frame-rate.md#draw-passes-that-wait-for-each-other-again-while-images-share-memory)). Also ruled out: mesh shaders writing only the vertices their primitives use (the highest index + 1, instead of all `max_v`), as an overlay of the 31 play mesh shaders against the same shaders recompiled: span 14.7 / 15.5 ms against 16.0 / 15.6, so the game's meshlets are full. A shader timeline needs the trace attached before the pipelines are made, and a recording from 3 s to the play window (9 GB, or 45 s with the shader profiler) never finished saving.
 - Measured on the M4 Pro (20-core GPU, 2026-11-08, `kokiri-deck`, one run each): at full resolution (the main passes 1536x864 in pass dumps at 75, 100 and 125 s) 48.1 fps, frame p50 16.74 / p95 33.41 ms, GPU p50 15.0 / p95 16.9 ms: the GPU needs about one vsync a frame, so the frame step holds 2 vsyncs much of the time. With the game's own dynamic resolution and the host fixes above: 60 fps, GPU p50 12.6 ms, at a lower resolution. A second dynamic-resolution run wandered off the route into Saria's dialogue and is not counted.
 
+## Wide screen
+
+- Current: with `DM_SCREEN_WIDE=3440x1440` (launcher setting *Wide screen*, **Match screen** by default on the Mac; offered because the rewrite declares the `SCREEN_WIDE` capability) the game renders a 3440x1440 picture instead of 2560x1440: the swap chain and the composite 3440x1440, the 3D targets 2580x1080 instead of 1920x1080, the scene at 16:9's vertical angle with more at the sides, the HUD at the screen's edges (hearts and item slots in the corners, the minimap bottom right), the menus' panels centred, their text and the items they render in 3D at their 16:9 shape (the menus' 2D pictures are stretched: Open, below). The engine does the layout by itself once its frame buffer is wider; the stubs in [`patches.py`](patches.py) (`screen_wide`, five functions found by fingerprint, all or none) change only what holds 16:9: nnMain's create arg (the swap chain's 2560x1440), the framework constructor's docked and handheld frame buffer sizes, and three camera updates that copy a 16/9 aspect of their own into the view's `sead::PerspectiveProjection` (aspect at +0xb0). With the sizes alone the 16:9 view was stretched over the wide picture. The host side is `loader.c` (state +0xa0 the ratio in 16.16, +0xa4 as a float; `auto`, the default for a build whose fixups table has `SCREEN_WIDE`, asks the front end for the main screen's size), `mtl_present_screen` for the picture's shape, and `front_mac.m` (the screen's size; a window of the picture's shape).
+- Checked on an M5 Pro MacBook Pro (48 GB, macOS 27.2, 2026-11-12, headless, a Goron City save): the title, the load menu, the save's first frames and a walk: 3440x1440 screenshots whose centre matches the 16:9 run's pixel for pixel in proportions (Link, the Goron and the pot the same size), the same red lines as at 16:9 (`stage 5 cb3 has no uniform buffer bound`, which 16:9 prints too), GPU about 11-12 ms a frame in Goron City. In a window (`auto`): `the main screen is 3440x1440`, a 1720x720 window, the present's picture shape 3440x1440. With `DM_SCREEN_WIDE` unset or `0` the rewrite runs the original instructions: 2560x1440, as before.
+- Open: the UI's 2D pictures are stretched over the wider picture, 1.34 times at 3440x1440: the pause menu's art and slots, the load menu's panels and save pictures, the minimap. Text, glyphs and the items a menu renders in 3D keep their shape. The UI is drawn straight to the swap chain with an orthographic projection of 2/1280 by 2/720 (its uniform block at heap+0x226BD178 on this build, heap offsets being the same from run to run; written through a pool alias, so `DM_PROBE_POLL` sees the writes and `DM_PROBE_WATCH` does not), so its 1280 units span the whole width. The game has no nn::ui2d layouts (no `lyt1` or `pan1` tags), so it is another UI system and Breath of the Wild's layout rules do not apply. Ruled out as the source of the 1280, each set to 1720 with no visible change: the framework's +0xb78 (`main+0x108b698`), the 13-slot create arg (`main+0xf26e48`), the display defaults (`main+0x369a444`), the TV display's virtual size (`main+0x6549d0`, which does change the layers' virtual canvas and five other orthographic matrices to 2/1720), six 1280x720 constants in `main+0x1537d48..0x1539ae0`, and the size-getter classes at `main+0x20ec4a0`.
+- Against [PR 1460](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1460), which widens the same three sizes at build time (an environment switch of the NSP script's run) together with the output width as a float (`main+0xecbc80`) and the output resolution table (`main+0x3f58540`, its 2560x1440 entry), and no camera: with those two also at 3440 on top of the stubs the pause menu's stretch and the play HUD did not change, so they are not among the stubs; with them and the three camera sites back at the original load, play in Goron City showed the 16:9 view stretched over the wide picture (Link, his shield and the Goron visibly wider), so the cameras are needed there (2026-11-12, words replaced in the rewritten main, one run each).
+- Stays 16:9 inside the wide picture: three targets made over and over in play, 2560x1440 (format 0x38), 1920x1080 (0x1) and 960x540 (0xd), created from `main+0x3b6345c`; not identified (the picture shows no 16:9 part). Open: handheld mode (`DM_OPMODE=0`) does not follow: the game crops its picture to 1920x1080 in the wide swap chain while the cameras take the wide ratio (a squeezed 16:9 picture in a corner), since its handheld size comes from somewhere the stubs do not reach (not the framework constructor's 1920x1080 at +0xb70, which the stub widens to no effect), so the loader keeps 16:9 in handheld mode. Not checked: cutscenes and movies (a movie is a 16:9 picture), the Deku Tree, a 32:9 screen, the frame rate against 16:9 on a bench route.
